{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Kecebas2017-benchmark-TiO2-SiO2-Ag",
  "Source": "M. A. Kecebas, M. P. Menguc, A. Kosar, and K. Sendur, \"Passive radiative cooling design with broadband optical thin-film filters,\" J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. (2017), article in press, pp. 1-8 (volume not printed in this copy). doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.03.046 - Sec. 2, p. 3 and Fig. 2(a): 'On Si substrate 50 nm thick Ag is coated. Then, 4 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 60 nm thick, are added. Finally 3 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 300 nm thick'; layer sequence from the Fig. 2(a) scheme",
  "Notes": "Benchmark passive daytime radiative-cooling coating (similar to Raman et al. 2014, ref. [3]): solar-reflecting thin segment on Ag plus thick mid-IR emitting segment\nSpecification: High reflectance 280 nm - 4 um (solar), high emissivity (absorptance) 8-13 um.\nReported: Average absorption ~3 % (reflection 97 %) over 280 nm - 4 um; average emission ~45 % in 8-13 um (Fig. 2 caption and p. 3). Fig. 2(b): absorption peak ~1.0 near 9.7-10 um, ~0.15-0.2 at 11-14 um (read off the plot, approximate). Cooling power 35 W/m^2 (p. 3), vs 40.1 +/- 4.1 W/m^2 reported in ref. [3].\nNotes: 7 dielectric layers, total 1140 nm (4 x 60 + 3 x 300). Order from Fig. 2(a): Ag, then TiO2/SiO2/TiO2/SiO2 at 60 nm, then TiO2/SiO2/TiO2 at 300 nm, TiO2 on top. The paper's stack is Si substrate / 50 nm Ag / dielectrics; Si has no index in the paper and is not an allowed reference, so the 50 nm Ag is treated as the (opaque) substrate. This is an approximation: Fig. 6(b) shows 50 nm Ag on Si reflecting ~97-98 % from ~0.5 to 13.5 um, so the Ag is effectively opaque in the IR, but a little light may reach the Si at short wavelengths. No optical constants are given for TiO2 (anatase), SiO2, Al2O3 or Ag (single-layer absorption is only plotted in Fig. 3(a)-(c)); database names are used, and their 8-13 um phonon absorption (the purpose of the absorption segment) may not be represented by generic dispersions, so the 8-13 um emission may not be reproducible. The paper lists the stack from the Ag upward (substrate first); it is reversed here so layer 1 is next to air. Paper averages are plain wavelength averages (Sec. 2, p. 3). Normal incidence unless stated; unpolarised assumed. Ag here is Yang et al. 2015 (refractiveindex.info, 0.27-24.9 um), which covers the infrared; the generic TiO2 and SiO2 have no infrared absorption, so the emission averages cannot be reproduced with this design - see the -MIR variants.\nLayers from Sec. 2, p. 3 and Fig. 2(a): 'On Si substrate 50 nm thick Ag is coated. Then, 4 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 60 nm thick, are added. Finally 3 layers of TiO2 and SiO2, each 300 nm thick'; layer sequence from the Fig. 2(a) scheme; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours mean R 97.6 % over 300-2500 nm (generic TiO2 and SiO2, Yang Ag); paper reflection 97 % over 280 nm - 4 um. Checked to 2500 nm only; the generic SiO2 model stops at 3.71 um and none of the generic oxides absorb in the infrared, so the emission figures need the -MIR variant.",
